I need help writing a function that merges two sorted lists into a single sorted list. The function should be written in C++. Here are the unit tests:

// Test 1: merge({1, 3, 5}, {2, 4, 6}) returns {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}
// Test 2: merge({1, 2, 3}, {}) returns {1, 2, 3}
// Test 3: merge({}, {4, 5, 6}) returns {4, 5, 6}
// Test 4: merge({1, 3, 5}, {1, 3, 5}) returns {1, 1, 3, 3, 5, 5}

answer:
Here's a C++ function that merges two sorted lists into a single sorted list:

```cpp
#include <vector>

std::vector<int> merge(const std::vector<int>& list1, const std::vector<int>& list2) {
    std::vector<int> result;
    size_t i = 0, j = 0;
    while (i < list1.size() && j < list2.size()) {
        if (list1[i] < list2[j]) {
            result.push_back(list1[i]);
            ++i;
        } else {
            result.push_back(list2[j]);
            ++j;
        }
    }
    while (i < list1.size()) {
        result.push_back(list1[i]);
        ++i;
    }
    while (j < list2.size()) {
        result.push_back(list2[j]);
        ++j;
    }
    return result;
}
```

This function uses a two-pointer technique to merge the lists efficiently. It iterates through both lists, adding the smaller current element to the result vector. After reaching the end of one list, it appends the remaining elements of the other list.

